L wrote:
> In this patch, the "finger of death" spell is changed such that,
> instead of firing a death ray, it instead makes your next few melee
> strikes instantly kill ("use the Touch of Death") those that it hits
> (unless they are immune to death spells) - similar to the "confuse
> monster" spell.
>
> Casting it at expert proficiency gives you 2-4 uses. Casting it at
> skilled proficiency gives you 2 uses. Casting it at lower proficiency,
> or when you already have 'uses' to expend, gives you 1 (extra) use.
>
> This patch also makes it easier to add more "touch" spells in the
> future.
>
> <> http://www.greyfire.org/~l/touch.diff <>

I like it, but have a few reservations. (Some have been mentioned
already.)

The main one is that it's the TOUCH of death, not the sword strike (or
whatever weapon is applicable) of death. You should actually have to
unwield your weapon and successfully hit the monster in hand-to-hand
combat in order for it to work. If you're not wearing gloves, of
course, you'd suffer the consequences of doing so (i.e. cockatrice
stoning) if applicable.

Perhaps an exception could be made for non-iron weapons (such that a
wizard might often use, like quarterstaffs and elven daggers and so
forth. Athames should probably be exempt as well.) I just can't see any
metal weapon transmitting a spell to the monster it touches, given that
metal is inimical to magic.

